{
	"nodes":[
		{"id":"a000000000000001","type":"group","x":0,"y":0,"width":1160,"height":860,"label":"1. Foundational Principle & Subconscious Direction","color":"6"},
		{"id":"a000000000000002","type":"group","x":1280,"y":-140,"width":1240,"height":1140,"label":"2. Spatial Gravity: Narrow Pockets vs. The Broad Canvas","color":"5"},
		{"id":"a000000000000003","type":"group","x":2640,"y":0,"width":1160,"height":860,"label":"3. Productive Friction, Exploration & Core Takeaways","color":"3"},
		{"id":"a000000000000004","type":"group","x":640,"y":1120,"width":2520,"height":820,"label":"4. Studio Collaboration & Production Lens (Audio & Tech)","color":"4"},
		{"id":"b100000000000001","type":"text","x":40,"y":60,"width":520,"height":340,"color":"6","text":"# Camera Distance from Player (or) PoI\n\n> *\"The eye trusts what stays steady while the world rearranges itself; move the lens by a whisper, and the mind rewrites the entire room without ever knowing why.\"*\n\n### Foundational Principle\n- Camera distance from the [[Player]] (or [[Point of Interest|PoI]]) is the most critical anchor of direction.\n- Amidst scenes full of constantly changing elements, camera distance must stay **consistent** so the player can intuitively learn and trust it."},
		{"id":"b100000000000002","type":"text","x":40,"y":460,"width":520,"height":340,"color":"2","text":"## Acute Visual Sensitivity\n\n- The viewer's eyes are acutely sensitive to camera movement.\n- Even tiny, microscopic movements vastly reshape how the scene reads and feels.\n- This sensitivity carries immense weight in both directions and requires deliberate, close-grained direction."},
		{"id":"b100000000000003","type":"text","x":620,"y":60,"width":500,"height":420,"color":"5","text":"## The #HitmanWoA Subconscious Benchmark\n\n- In *Hitman: World of Assassination* (`#HitmanWoA`), the camera adjusts distance **very subtly and slowly** while the point of focus (generally the [[Player]]) remains steady.\n- **Low Perceptual Stimulus:** Dampens the jolt of perspective change so the camera never gets in the way of experiencing the scene.\n- **Subconscious Reconfiguration:** Quietly shifts the player's entire cognitive processing system while the player barely notices the camera moved."},
		{"id":"b100000000000004","type":"text","x":620,"y":540,"width":500,"height":260,"text":"### Visual Reference: Subtle Distance Shift\n\n![[close_camera_moving_far.gif]]\n\n*`<a gif i have taken of close camera moving far>`*\nSlow pull-back while keeping the player focal anchor steady."},
		{"id":"b200000000000001","type":"text","x":1320,"y":-60,"width":340,"height":260,"color":"5","text":"## Spatial Gravity\n\nThe physical geometry of the space sets the gravity; camera distance adapts around it to guide how the brain processes information."},
		{"id":"b200000000000002","type":"text","x":1720,"y":-80,"width":440,"height":440,"color":"4","text":"## 1. Narrow / Enclosed Space (Close Camera)\n\n- When entering a narrow or closed space, the camera stays **close** (`#MicroFocus`).\n- **Generous Screen Real Estate:** Nearby items occupy a large portion of the frame with very few competing elements.\n- **Detail-Oriented Agency:** The player naturally pays close attention to immediate surroundings—choosing to see, examine, and interact far more often."},
		{"id":"b200000000000003","type":"text","x":2200,"y":-80,"width":280,"height":440,"color":"4","text":"### Close Framing Capture\n\n![[close_camera_examinable_items.png]]\n\n*`<a picture i have taken of close camera but also there is more items that are worth looking and examining>`*\n\nHigh screen real estate per item; low peripheral noise."},
		{"id":"b200000000000004","type":"text","x":1720,"y":440,"width":440,"height":500,"color":"2","text":"## 2. Open Space (Far Camera)\n\n- As the player enters an open space, the camera slowly pulls back onto a **broad canvas** (`#MacroCanvas`).\n- **Broad Scene Reading:** Focus shifts from inspecting close details to understanding what is happening across the entire scene.\n- **Screen-Space Competition:** Items sit further away with smaller screen real estate, and dozens of other elements simultaneously ask for attention.\n- **Natural Grain:** The far camera still *allows* close examination, though doing so works against what the lens naturally encourages."},
		{"id":"b200000000000005","type":"text","x":2200,"y":440,"width":280,"height":500,"color":"2","text":"### Far Framing Capture\n\n![[far_camera_broad_canvas.png]]\n\n*`<a picture i have taken of far camera>`*\n\nWide situational awareness; high visual competition across the canvas."},
		{"id":"b300000000000001","type":"text","x":2680,"y":60,"width":520,"height":420,"color":"1","text":"## Productive Friction & Active Exploration\n\n- The difficulty/discomfort of isolating specific details inside a wide, busy frame is **essential for the player to explore the experience**.\n- Over-directing every beat makes the player passive and overly comfortable with only one prescribed mode of receiving the world.\n- Perceptual friction pushes the player to learn the space and take **active steps** to investigate.\n- *==THE PLAYER'S ACTIVE EXPLORATION IS THE MAIN EXPERIENCE==* (`#PlayerAgency`)."},
		{"id":"b300000000000002","type":"text","x":3260,"y":60,"width":500,"height":420,"color":"3","text":"## Core Takeaways & Immersion\n\n1. **Constancy Builds Trust:** Camera distance is the learnable anchor across shifting scenes.\n2. **Imperceptible Shifts:** Slow distance gradients around a steady [[Player]] focal point shift cognition subconsciously (`#HitmanWoA`).\n3. **Real Estate Shapes Behavior:** Close = tactile inspection (`#MicroFocus`); Far = macro scene awareness (`#MacroCanvas`).\n4. **Friction Fuels Agency:** Resisting hand-holding invites active exploration (`#SubconsciousDirection`)."},
		{"id":"b300000000000003","type":"text","x":2680,"y":540,"width":1080,"height":260,"text":"### Other notes (Verbatim Director Seeds)\n> - \"The viewer eyes are very sensitive to the camera movement. Even tiny movements influence a very vastly to the scene. which can be both good and bad, but needs direction very closely.\"\n> - \"I'd say the far camera still allows what the close camera does, but its just not what it is naturally\"\n> - \"if we carefully direct everything, this will lead the player being too comfortable on just one way of what a direction design is offering. the player should explore that is the Main experence.\""},
		{"id":"b400000000000001","type":"text","x":680,"y":1180,"width":620,"height":440,"color":"4","text":"## Music Designer & Audio Director Lens\n\n- **Acoustic Palette (Close vs. Far):**\n  - *Close Camera:* Intimate dry early reflections, tactile material sounds (stone, wood, cloth), clear mid-highs for nearby objects.\n  - *Far Camera:* Smooth expansion into wide environmental diffusion and ambient beds while keeping the center channel clean.\n- **Vertical Layering & Diegetic Breadcrumbs:**\n  - Keep a constant root drone/solo stem anchored to the [[Player]] while harmonic pads widen on the exact same curve as the camera arm.\n  - Use subtle diegetic spatial anchors in the far camera to reward players who actively walk closer.\n- **Audio J-Cut & Dharma Restraint:**\n  - Swell open-space ambiance by 1–2 dB ~0.5s *before* the camera pulls back so the ear primes the eye.\n  - Avoid loud musical stingers that force attention in wide scenes."},
		{"id":"b400000000000002","type":"text","x":680,"y":1660,"width":620,"height":240,"color":"4","text":"### Director-to-Composer Brief\n1. *What single root stem can stay unchanged as the camera pulls back so the ear stays anchored to the character?*\n2. *How can subtle diegetic sounds reward active proximity in wide scenes without shouting for attention?*\n3. *How should we weight Wwise/FMOD listener attenuation between camera and pawn during distance shifts?*"},
		{"id":"b400000000000003","type":"text","x":1900,"y":1180,"width":620,"height":440,"color":"5","text":"## Technical Director Lens\n\n- **Spatial Volumes & Spring-Arm Interpolation:**\n  - Drive distance via `CameraPocketVolume` (`Narrow_Close`, `Standard_Baseline`, `Open_Canvas`) using a critically damped spring over a slow 2.5–4.0s curve.\n  - Lock the focal socket steadily on the [[Player]] or active [[Point of Interest|PoI]].\n- **Screen-Space Interaction Scaling:**\n  - Gate inspection prompts by both world distance and projected screen-space bounding box size so wide scenes stay free of icon clutter.\n- **HLOD Pre-Warming & Listener Tether:**\n  - Pre-warm HLODs at transition thresholds so slow pull-backs never cause visible mesh pop-in.\n  - Weight the audio listener (75% Player Pawn / 25% Camera) so footsteps stay grounded."},
		{"id":"b400000000000004","type":"text","x":1900,"y":1660,"width":620,"height":240,"color":"5","text":"### Director-to-Tech Handoff\n1. **Zero Focal Jitter:** Smooth ease-in/ease-out arm interpolation while keeping the player's screen-space anchor rock-solid.\n2. **No UI Hand-Holding in Far Camera:** Suppress distant interaction markers in open spaces so the player must actively step closer to inspect."},
		{"id":"b400000000000005","type":"text","x":1360,"y":1340,"width":480,"height":260,"color":"6","text":"## Production Alignment Core\n\n**Keep complexity in the player's perception, never in brittle code.**\n\nAudio attenuation, spring-arm lag, and screen-space culling must move in lockstep on the same slow interpolation curve."}
	],
